s:worthless"},{"word":"wutless","source":"Thesaurus:worthless","tags":["Caribbean"]}],"glosses":["Having no real substance, value, or importance; empty; void; worthless; unsatisfying."],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"id":"079c85f8-5689-4c2b-80ba-661b22b7265d"},{"examples":[{"text":"vain toil    a vain attempt","bold_text_offsets":[[0,4],[15,19]],"type":"example","id":"de4d0fd8-6d4a-43ed-a24b-39442704cbb5"},{"text":"Vain is the force of man / To crush the pillars which the pile sustain.","ref":"1697, Virgil, “(please specify the book number)”, in John Dryden, transl., The Works of Virgil: Containing His Pastorals, Georgics, and Æneis. […], London: […] Jacob Tonson, […], →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[0,4]],"id":"25d000c9-5a66-48f2-829d-377f91a1af36"},{"text":"At our old pastimes in the hall\n⁠We gambol’d, making vain pretence\n⁠Of gladness, with an awful sense\nOf one mute Shadow watching all.","ref":"1850, [Alfred, Lord Tennyson], “Canto XXX”, in In Memoriam, London: Edward Moxon, […], →OCLC, page 48:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[53,57]],"id":"b2a677ee-770c-404e-a206-85eef2850512"},{"text":"But Sophia's mother was not the woman to brook defiance. After a few moments' vain remonstrance her husband complied. His manner and appearance were suggestive of a satiated sea-lion.","ref":"1921, Ben Travers, chapter 6, in A Cuckoo in the Nest, Garden City, N.Y.: Doubleday, Page & Company, published 1925, →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[78,82]],"id":"ae076b93-ab4f-4ee4-aea2-ec4bb103455f"}],"links":[["futile","futile"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"pointless"},{"word":"futile"},{"word":"fruitless"},{"word":"ineffectual"},{"word":"bootless","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"barren","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"futile","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"fruitless","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"gainless","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"idle","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"inane","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"ineffectual","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"inefficacious","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"inutile","source":"Thesaurus:futile","tags":["obsolete"]},{"word":"no good","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"otiose","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"pointless","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"profitless","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"purposeless","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"tired","source":"Thesaurus:futile","tags":["African-American-Vernacular-English","derogatory"]},{"word":"unavailing","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"unprofitable","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"unuseful","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"unutilizable","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"useless","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"vain","source":"Thesaurus:futile"},{"word":"void","source":"Thesaurus:futile"}],"glosses":["Effecting no purpose; pointless, futile."],"categories":["English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples"],"id":"25c5bcdf-ae41-4ee6-b692-e537228804a8"},{"examples":[{"text":"Yet ſhall (my Lord) your juſt, your noble Rules / Fill half the land with imitating Fools: / VVho random dravvings from your ſheets ſhall take, / And of one beauty many blunders make; / Load ſome vain Church with old Theatric State, / Turn Arcs of Triumph to a Garden-gate, […]","ref":"1735, Alexander Pope, “Epistle 4”, in The Works of Mr. Alexander Pope, volume II, London: […] J. Wright, for Lawton Gilliver […], →OCLC, page 40, lines 25–30:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[196,200]],"id":"4369efb0-f7e9-41f3-9579-b5a6a4cb9e26"}],"links":[["Showy","showy"],["ostentatious","ostentatious"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"brash"},{"word":"pizazzy"},{"word":"bedizened","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy","tags":["literary"]},{"word":"blatant","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"blingish","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"brash","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"campy","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"catchpenny","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"chichi","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"chintzy","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"corny","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"crass","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"crude","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"flashy","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"flaunting","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"florid","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"flossy","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"froufrou","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"garish","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"gauche","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"gaudy","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"gay","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"glaring","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"gross","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"gussied up","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"hideous","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"kitsch","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"kitschy","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"loud","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"meretricious","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"naff","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"obtrusive","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"ostentatious","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"pizazzy","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"pretentious","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"ritzy","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"raffish","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"schlocky","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"showy","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"snazzy","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"splendiferous","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"tacky","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"tasteless","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"tawdry","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"tinselly","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"too much","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"trinkety","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"unbecoming","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"untasteful","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"vain","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"whorish","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"},{"word":"zesty","source":"Thesaurus:gaudy"}],"glosses":["Showy; ostentatious."],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"id":"5019acf3-a623-44df-a724-fd804276028e"}],"related":[{"word":"vanity"},{"word":"one-dimensional","source":"Thesaurus:vain"},{"word":"shallow","source":"Thesaurus:vain"},{"word":"superficial","source":"Thesaurus:vain"},{"word":"arrogance","source":"Thesaurus:vain"},{"word":"beauty","source":"Thesaurus:vain"}],"synonyms":[{"word":"conceited","source":"Thesaurus:vain"},{"word":"narcissistic","source":"Thesaurus:vain"},{"word":"self-adoring","source":"Thesaurus:vain"},{"word":"self-admiring","source":"Thesaurus:vain"},{"word":"self-obsessed","source":"Thesaurus:vain"},{"word":"vain","source":"Thesaurus:vain"},{"word":"vainglorious","source":"Thesaurus:vain"}]},"vainglory":{"id":"4d9e36d8-612c-4940-84d0-a7f3b2c894a9","word":"vainglory","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"excessive vanity","tags":["feminine"],"word":"vanglória"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"boastful, unwarranted pride","tags":["feminine"],"word":"vanglória"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"vain, ostentatious display","tags":["feminine"],"word":"vanglória"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"a regarding of oneself with undue favor","tags":["feminine"],"word":"vanglória"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"links":[["vanity","vanity"]],"glosses":["Excessive vanity."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"1dde5dd4-bde6-454a-b8ca-4524143f4768"},{"examples":[{"text":"Then he reasoned, as he had in the days past, that the course of human history had shown national pride—the inflexible kind—to be mankind’s worst enemy, and ordinary people paid the price in suffering. Nations had gone down because of vainglory, when moderation might have civilized and saved them.","ref":"1962, Arthur Hailey, “Chapter Eleven: The White House”, in In High Places (fiction):","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[235,244]],"id":"c6f53d48-cbb9-4fb7-9315-fa1c237ad373"}],"links":[["Boastful","boastful"],["pride","pride"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"glosses":["Boastful, unwarranted pride in one's accomplishments or qualities."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"25daab74-ee5e-4096-b06e-c0586193cf3b"},{"examples":[{"text":"The pew would soon want new flooring, Mr. Dangerfield thought, and the Castlemallard arms and supporters, a rather dingy piece of vainglory, overhanging the main seat on the wall, would be nothing the worse of a little fresh gilding and paint.","ref":"1863, Sheridan Le Fanu, The House by the Churchyard:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[130,139]],"id":"64bab1c0-d08d-4a96-8723-c33d7a5b1bcc"}],"links":[["Vain","vain"],["ostentatious","ostentatious"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"glosses":["Vain, ostentatious display."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"e8fe5bd1-6c14-4ff6-8367-cfe341609f4e"},{"links":[["favor","favor"]],"glosses":["A regarding of oneself with undue favor."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"a17b47dd-5867-4cde-a72c-10f5c983d060"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"vainglorious":{"id":"4418681a-c488-48b1-adcc-a184ab13609b","word":"vainglorious","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"with excessive vanity or unwarranted pride","tags":["masculine"],"word":"vanglorioso"}],"pos":"adj","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"And the Apoſtle [Paul] ſeems here moſt peculiarly to have directed this Encomium of the Gospel, as a Defiance to the Philoſophers of his Time, the Fluſtring Vain-glorious Greeks, vvho pretended ſo much to magnify, and even Adore the VViſdom they profeſſed, […]","ref":"1694 May 8 (date delivered; Gregorian calendar); first published 1698, Robert South, “Christianity Mysterious, and the Wisdom of God in Making it so, Proved in a Sermon Preached at Westminster-Abbey, April 29. 1694.”, in Twelve Sermons upon Several Subjects and Occasions, volume III, London: […] Tho[mas] Warren for Thomas Bennet […], →OCLC, page 263:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[157,170]],"id":"906be2a0-79e3-4d94-9caa-07d773c531f5"},{"text":"I sat in the sun on a bench; the animal within me licking the chops of memory; the spiritual side a little drowsed, promising subsequent penitence, but not yet moved to begin. After all, I reflected I was like my neighbours; and then I smiled, comparing myself with other men, comparing my active goodwill with the lazy cruelty of their neglect. And at the very moment of that vainglorious thought, a qualm came over me, a horrid nausea and the most deadly shuddering.","ref":"1886 January 5, Robert Louis Stevenson, “Henry Jekyll’s Full Statement of the Case”, in Strange Case of Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde, London: Longmans, Green, and Co., →OCLC, page 131:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[377,389]],"id":"537b2ba5-962f-438f-b60e-b792387b64cc"},{"text":"\"So kindly keep the vainglorious enumeration of your pots for the benefit of those village idiots who compose your particular set of boozing companions.\"","ref":"1930, Norman Lindsay, Redheap, Sydney, N.S.W.: Ure Smith, →OCLC, page 85:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[20,32]],"id":"e025329e-ee66-446d-9078-7e1cbbde7105"},{"text":"Railway engines have been attacked with gunfire by raiding aircraft on both sides of the Channel and the impression has grown up that they are defenceless monsters to be pestered with impunity. The first engine to disprove this vainglorious theory was, we are glad to note, a British one. [The locomotive boiler exploded, causing the aircraft to crash.]","ref":"1943 March and April, “Notes and News: Southern Locomotive Destroys Raider”, in Railway Magazine, page 119:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[228,240]],"id":"da421087-003a-4f1a-9f14-3522d945253f"},{"text":"These attacks follow a familiar pattern: Their targets represent a threat to Trump’s vainglorious self-image.","ref":"2016 February 29, Ron Fournier, “How to Bully a Bully”, in The Atlantic, archived from the original on 05 Jul 2016:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[85,97]],"id":"97663a51-fbb8-4842-b58e-9f4ec5c5e1d9"}],"links":[["vanity","vanity"],["pride","pride"]],"categories":["English adjectives","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English terms derived from Latin","English terms derived from Middle English","English terms derived from Old French","English terms inherited from Middle English","English terms with quotations","Entries with translation boxes","Mandarin terms with non-redundant manual transliterations","Pages with 1 entry","Pages with entries","Rhymes:English/ɔːɹiəs","Rhymes:English/ɔːɹiəs/4 syllables","Terms with Ancient Greek translations","Terms with Armenian translations","Terms with Bulgarian translations","Terms with Czech translations","Terms with Dutch translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with Georgian translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Gothic translations","Terms with Greek translations","Terms with Italian translations","Terms with Mandarin translations","Terms with Middle French translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Spanish translations","en:Personality"],"glosses":["Possessing excessive vanity or unwarranted pride."],"id":"db5d0d83-d2e4-43cb-95e6-f6dd4889e171"}],"related":[{"word":"vainglory"}],"synonyms":[]},"vair":{"id":"1f345845-0244-4874-86a1-4b189ee92731","word":"vair","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"heraldic fur","tags":["masculine"],"word":"veiro"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Bran wore grey breeches and white doublet, his sleeves and collar trimmed with vair.","ref":"1999, George R.R. Martin, A Clash of Kings, Bantam, published 2011, page 323:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[79,83]],"id":"d2350a17-6c18-4453-8099-6961f458dbfd"}],"links":[["fur","fur"],["squirrel","squirrel"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations","Quotation templates to be cleaned"],"glosses":["A type of fur from a squirrel with a grey back and white belly, much used on garments in the Middle Ages."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"4cb1f2da-f7ce-4b34-831c-001e60c87406"},{"topics":["government","heraldry","hobbies","lifestyle","monarchy","nobility","politics"],"categories":["en:Heraldic tinctures"],"links":[["heraldry","heraldry"],["heraldic","heraldic"],["fur","fur"],["tessellation","tessellation"],["bell","bell"]],"raw_glosses":["(heraldry) An heraldic fur formed by a regular tessellation of bell shapes in two colours, (for example in the image, blue and white)."],"glosses":["An heraldic fur formed by a regular tessellation of bell shapes in two colours, (for example in the image, blue and white)."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"f3dc8760-2ba3-487a-9ac6-1dc7971079db"}],"related":[{"word":"countervair"},{"word":"vairy"},{"word":"varriated"},{"word":"ermine"},{"word":"fur"},{"word":"potent"}],"synonyms":[]},"vaishya":{"id":"965e2e92-c96d-4e86-86a8-aacf2501df04","word":"Vaishya","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"caste","tags":["feminine","masculine"],"word":"vaixá"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"links":[["merchants","merchants"],["landowners","landowners"]],"glosses":["The third caste of traditional Indian society, comprising merchants and landowners."],"id":"97f88000-22c4-43e3-aa81-3afa00e07a66"},{"examples":[{"text":"To perform successfully in the marketplace, vaishyas had to be ambitious, to want worldly goods, and to compete aggressively with their rivals, and this \"desire\" bound them inexorably to the cycle of death and rebirth.","ref":"2006, Karen Armstrong, The Great Transformation, Atlantic Books, published 2007, page 307:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[44,52]],"id":"f3a05cee-704e-4b55-9a9c-03547e4ef0cf"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"glosses":["A member of this caste."],"id":"b226e08f-c207-403a-b931-8ed22fd27d76"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"vaisheshika":{"id":"68b2637e-034d-43c0-81ac-067041d95d51","word":"Vaisheshika","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"school of Hindu philosophy","word":"Vaisesica"}],"pos":"name","tags":null,"senses":[{"links":[["school","school"],["Hindu","Hindu"],["philosophy","philosophy"],["ancient","ancient"],["India","India"]],"glosses":["A particular school of Hindu philosophy from ancient India."],"categories":["English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English proper nouns","English terms borrowed from Sanskrit","English terms derived from Sanskrit","English transliterations of Sanskrit terms","English uncountable nouns","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 1 entry","Pages with entries","Terms with Bengali translations","Terms with Bhojpuri translations","Terms with Cantonese translations","Terms with Dutch translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Hindi translations","Terms with Italian translations","Terms with Japanese translations","Terms with Kannada translations","Terms with Korean translations","Terms with Malayalam translations","Terms with Mandarin translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Punjabi translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Sanskrit translations","Terms with Spanish translations","Terms with Tamil translations","en:Hinduism"],"id":"7a121cde-0ee7-4cae-80cb-7e3429e8428b"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]}}
